<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carlota of Mexico</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1840–1927</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Charlotte of Belgium, Empress Carlota</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carlota of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, born <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princess-charlotte-of-wales">Princess Charlotte</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, was the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-i-of-belgium">Leopold I of Belgium</a> and the wife of Maximilian, with whom she reigned as empress of Mexico from 1864 to 1867. In 1866 she travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to seek help for the failing empire from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix">Pope Pius IX</a>, but was refused and suffered a mental breakdown. She spent the rest of her long life in seclusion in Belgium.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carlota-of-mexico.jpg" alt="Carlota of Mexico" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: J. Malovich, Trieste · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carlota_de_mejico.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
